WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.629 --> 00:00:04.780
[LAUGHS]

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:09.850
Want me to get it?

00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:13.833
Oh man, that's beautiful!

00:00:13.833 --> 00:00:15.500
Now I've got butter
running down my arm,

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:16.950
and that's always a good thing.

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:19.777
This bread is delicious.

00:00:19.777 --> 00:00:21.360
So Tim, we're all
going to be spending

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:23.220
a lot more time at
home, it's fair to say,

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:25.440
over the coming
weeks and months.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:28.830
It's now the time to get
back to proper home cooking.

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:31.980
It's important to have
a few basic things that

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:33.145
might be useful.

00:00:33.145 --> 00:00:34.770
I thought today we'd
start off by doing

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:37.462
a really simple loaf of bread.

00:00:37.462 --> 00:00:38.420
Have you got your gear?

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:40.140
I've got a mixing bowl, yeast.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:40.890
Real yeast, right?

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:41.600
Proper yeast?

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:43.035
This is fresh yeast.

00:00:43.035 --> 00:00:46.065
All I've got is dried yeast, but
they're roughly the same thing.

00:00:46.065 --> 00:00:47.440
So get your bowl
on your scale...

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:48.165
Yep.

00:00:48.165 --> 00:00:50.980
...and then just weigh into
that 500 grammes of flour.

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:52.980
I've often heard people
say baking is a science.

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:54.630
You have to really be precise.

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:56.090
Oh yes, absolutely.

00:00:56.090 --> 00:00:58.950
All that stove top nonsense with
flame and fire, that's easy.

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:01.140
This stuff is serious science.

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:02.940
I'm using strong bread flour.

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:05.393
I had to hunt high and low
for this strong bread flour.

00:01:05.393 --> 00:01:06.810
It's not everywhere
at the moment.

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:09.210
People have emptied the
shelves in a lot of places.

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:11.800
If I had another type of flour,
could I do something similar,

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:13.383
or does it have to
be the bread flour?

00:01:13.383 --> 00:01:15.840
You can do this with general
purpose regular flour,

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:17.490
not self-raising,
but regular flour.

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:18.570
You can do it.

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:22.243
You probably won't get as nice
a crust or as nice a crumb,

00:01:22.243 --> 00:01:24.660
but you'll be able to get a
perfectly working, serviceable

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:25.530
loaf.

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:30.180
I want to measure 7 grammes
of the yeast in my case, 15

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:31.070
grammes in your case.

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:32.755
15 because I'm using
the fresh yeast.

00:01:32.755 --> 00:01:34.310
...onto one side of the flour.

00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:37.630
Now, where I'm pouring
mine, you crumble yours

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:39.990
onto the other side, pour
in 10 grammes of salt,

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:44.130
and weigh 350 grammes
of a tepid water right

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:45.380
into the middle of that flour.

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:47.735
Now, you are going to get
really floury and messy.

00:01:47.735 --> 00:01:49.110
You're going to
use your fingers.

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:51.073
Mix it and blend it.

00:01:51.073 --> 00:01:52.740
This is where it gets
therapeutic there.

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:55.120
This is still feeling
quite wet in my fingers.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:56.148
Is that all right?

00:01:56.148 --> 00:01:56.940
This does stay wet.

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:57.898
Don't worry about that.

00:01:57.898 --> 00:01:59.610
Now, here's a really
weird question.

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:01.195
Do you travel much with work?

00:02:01.195 --> 00:02:02.820
Well, not at the moment.

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:04.440
In the past, I used to, yes.

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:07.282
Are you one of those people
that nicks the shower caps?

00:02:07.282 --> 00:02:08.699
I don't think I've
ever done that.

00:02:08.699 --> 00:02:11.199
See, I'm the only bald man in
the world who nicks the shower

00:02:11.199 --> 00:02:12.990
caps, but the shower
caps in hotels

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:15.050
are really, really good
for covering dough.

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:17.910
Then you can see inside how
the dough's rising, but listen,

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:19.770
don't worry about that.

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:20.833
I've just admitted that.

00:02:20.833 --> 00:02:23.250
At least my stockpiles are of
something relatively benign.

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:24.740
Cover it up with the
a piece of damp cloth.

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:26.700
Over the next hour or so,
come down and check it,

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:28.908
and see if it's sort of
starting to move and puff up.

00:02:30.955 --> 00:02:32.510
Just having a look
at this thing,

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:34.508
it looks kind of gelatinous.

00:02:34.508 --> 00:02:35.800
Should I be worried about that?

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:37.092
No, that's really, really good.

00:02:37.092 --> 00:02:39.092
You know, when people
think about bread usually,

00:02:39.092 --> 00:02:40.600
they think about
kneading it a lot.

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:43.142
This gelatinous quality means
that the gluten's breaking down

00:02:43.142 --> 00:02:44.503
without having to be beaten up.

00:02:44.503 --> 00:02:46.420
What you need to do now
is get your hands wet.

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:49.300
Turn it, and kind of fold
it on itself a few times,

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:52.600
stretching the dough and
lifting it into the middle

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:54.450
and making a little tight ball.

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:56.200
And then put the cover
back on to leave it

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:57.200
for a couple more hours.

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:58.720
The more times you
stretch and fold,

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:00.910
the stronger the gluten
becomes, and therefore,

00:03:00.910 --> 00:03:03.243
when it puffs up, it's really
going to go like a rocket.

00:03:03.243 --> 00:03:06.250
Think about it as a sort
of a day-long process.

00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:08.650
How is your dough looking?

00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:10.858
It's looking pretty firm.

00:03:10.858 --> 00:03:11.650
Oh, don't shake it!

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:12.810
Don't shake it!

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:14.710
No, no.

00:03:14.710 --> 00:03:15.845
That's looking pretty good.

00:03:15.845 --> 00:03:16.720
Is that looking good?

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:17.330
That's good.

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:18.390
Stop joggling it about!

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:21.373
You'll let all the air out!

00:03:21.373 --> 00:03:23.290
I'm being a little bit
too enthusiastic there.

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:24.410
Be gentle, gentle with it.

00:03:24.410 --> 00:03:25.480
Now watch.

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:26.790
Here we go.

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:28.600
Take the lid off, and check it.

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:29.980
My lord, that looks beautiful.

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:30.610
Yes.

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:33.250
You need to create some enclosed
environment in which it can

00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:35.060
cook hot and hard and quickly.

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:37.135
So we're going to do it
inside one of these pots.

00:03:37.135 --> 00:03:39.310
Why one of these pots
and not kind of a more

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:40.452
conventional bread tin?

00:03:40.452 --> 00:03:41.410
A bread tin would work.

00:03:41.410 --> 00:03:42.730
You could easily put
it into a bread tin,

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:43.813
but it won't be so pretty.

00:03:43.813 --> 00:03:45.113
Put a squirt of oil into it.

00:03:45.113 --> 00:03:46.780
We're going to put a
little bit of flour

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:48.730
on the table in front of us.

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:51.460
Pour the dough out, the kind
of crusty top of the dough

00:03:51.460 --> 00:03:54.320
goes down onto the flour,
and the wet underside

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:56.700
stays upright.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:58.810
Reach it onto the far side.

00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:01.350
You put your fingers
underneath the dough, kind

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:04.753
of lift it and fold in
like little rabbit ears,

00:04:04.753 --> 00:04:06.670
then you turn it round
and pull in rabbit ears

00:04:06.670 --> 00:04:09.070
on the other two corners.

00:04:09.070 --> 00:04:11.320
So you're pulling these
little things into the centre.

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:14.076
And if it sticks a bit,
put a bit more flour dough.

00:04:14.076 --> 00:04:16.560
Do you see how it's sort of
tightening up into a ball?

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:18.060
Once you've got it
reasonably tight,

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:20.160
and it's kind of holding
together, chuck a bit

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:24.570
flour in it, flip it over
so the neat side is upwards.

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:25.952
Watch this movement.

00:04:25.952 --> 00:04:27.910
So you're kind of getting
your fingers under it

00:04:27.910 --> 00:04:28.600
and turning it.

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:29.500
You're tightening it.

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:32.830
And you can probably already
see in the surface of it

00:04:32.830 --> 00:04:34.930
that there's little
bubbles forming and stuff

00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:35.860
happening underneath.

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:37.540
But that stretchy
skin you're making

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:39.760
is what's going to hold
the bugger together.

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:43.450
Finally, put your hands
right underneath it,

00:04:43.450 --> 00:04:47.690
pick it up like that, move it
over the top of your Le Creuset

00:04:47.690 --> 00:04:50.868
and gently drop it
inside just like putting

00:04:50.868 --> 00:04:51.660
a baby in the bath.

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:53.451
And shall I push it out a
little bit so it fits there,

00:04:53.451 --> 00:04:53.925
or just leave it?

00:04:53.925 --> 00:04:54.060
No!

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:54.810
Don't you dare!

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:55.310
No.

00:04:55.310 --> 00:04:56.270
Quite the opposite.

00:04:56.270 --> 00:04:56.980
No.

00:04:56.980 --> 00:04:58.280
Let it do that by itself.

00:04:58.280 --> 00:04:59.885
Let it expand
against the outside,

00:04:59.885 --> 00:05:00.980
do you see what I mean?

00:05:00.980 --> 00:05:05.630
And when it's doubled, we slash
it and put it in a hot oven.

00:05:05.630 --> 00:05:07.700
The bread has risen beautifully.

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:08.527
Yours looks great.

00:05:08.527 --> 00:05:09.985
It looks better
than mine actually.

00:05:09.985 --> 00:05:10.485
Really?

00:05:10.485 --> 00:05:12.730
Mine's still quite
flat, yeah, but I've

00:05:12.730 --> 00:05:14.740
used a bigger pan than yours.

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:16.840
So mine's probably
too large a one.

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:19.460
Cut a big slash in the
top so the bread opens up

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:20.885
and the cross comes
up through it.

00:05:20.885 --> 00:05:21.920
I'm a bit nervous
doing this because it's

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:24.310
kind of stretching and
deflating the dough somewhat.

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:24.810
That's fine.

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:25.310
Don't worry.

00:05:25.310 --> 00:05:28.280
Take your glass of water,
open the oven door,

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:30.740
put this inside
right at the centre,

00:05:30.740 --> 00:05:34.640
and then throw the half
glass of water into the oven,

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:36.320
not into the pot.

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:40.070
Steam transmits the heat a
lot faster than just dry air.

00:05:40.070 --> 00:05:42.143
Also the steam gelatinises
is the outside.

00:05:42.143 --> 00:05:44.060
It gives it that lovely
sort of rubbery finish

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:46.640
which then crisps
off as it bakes.

00:05:49.545 --> 00:05:50.980
OK, it's the moment of truth.

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:56.810
And it smells fantastic.

00:05:59.805 --> 00:06:01.425
Oh man, that's beautiful.

00:06:01.425 --> 00:06:02.580
Yeah?

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:03.542
How does that look?

00:06:03.542 --> 00:06:04.840
That is staggeringly good.

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:06.976
OK, so shake it a bit.

00:06:06.976 --> 00:06:08.350
Is it loose in the pot?

00:06:08.350 --> 00:06:08.850
Yeah.

00:06:08.850 --> 00:06:11.130
Just basically turn
your loaf out onto it.

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:12.292
There goes mine.

00:06:12.292 --> 00:06:14.515
Out it goes.

00:06:14.515 --> 00:06:16.350
Oh yes!

00:06:16.350 --> 00:06:19.480
Oh mate, that is just superb!

00:06:23.505 --> 00:06:26.165
It's making a satisfying noise.

00:06:26.165 --> 00:06:27.983
That's a fabulous sound.

00:06:27.983 --> 00:06:29.650
Now mine's actually
come out with a more

00:06:29.650 --> 00:06:32.010
sort of foccacia-like crumb.

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:33.820
Mine would have been
good in a smaller tin,

00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:35.112
so it would have raised higher.

00:06:35.112 --> 00:06:36.050
That's not bad.

00:06:36.050 --> 00:06:37.990
There's a good
distribution in that,

00:06:37.990 --> 00:06:40.210
so all the little air
bubbles have gone through it.

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:41.890
What do you reckon?

00:06:41.890 --> 00:06:43.310
Pretty good.

00:06:43.310 --> 00:06:43.810
Yeah.

00:06:43.810 --> 00:06:45.745
Salty butter or unsalted
butter are you using?

00:06:45.745 --> 00:06:46.720
Salty of course.

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:47.530
Has to be.

00:06:47.530 --> 00:06:51.250
I also find it incredibly
rewarding as well as delicious.

00:06:51.250 --> 00:06:53.880
Oh, now I've got butter
running down my arm,

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:56.450
and that's always a good thing.

